{#
   THE calendar board — one component, every calendar surface (S146a).

   🔴 **What it replaces.** `calendrier.html.twig` and `machine-calendrier.html.twig`
   each carried their own copy of the same grid and the same twelve JavaScript
   functions, and in the single session S134d/S134e the same opening-hours rule had
   to be edited in both files four times over. The logic now lives once, in
   `assets/controllers/calendar_controller.js`; the payload is built once, in
   `App\Calendar\CalendarPayload`; this file is the markup, once.

   ⚠️ **The controller element is the PAGE's, not this partial's.** The stats cards,
   the resource filter panel and the booking dialog are all targets of the same
   `calendar` controller, and Stimulus only sees targets inside the controlled
   element — so the page puts `data-controller="calendar"` on its shell and includes
   this. Wrapping this file in its own controller would cut the board off from the
   dialog it opens.

   ⚠️ **The location tiles are server-rendered links, not a client filter.** Opening
   hours, exceptions and the bookable resources all change with the location, and
   they come from the server; a client-side location toggle would show one location's
   week over another location's machines. They also carry the current view, so
   switching location does not silently drop the month.

   Parameters:
     heading, subtitle  the board's own words — the page decides them
     venue_context      optional; the array from `VenueContext::forRequest()`
     venue_route        route the location tiles link to (required with venue_context)
     venue_query        current query string, re-emitted on every tile
     booking            whether slots are clickable at all (S146c turns this off).
                        ⚠️ It changes the LEGEND's wording as well as nothing else here:
                        the grid's own read-only behaviour comes from the payload, not
                        from this flag. Keep the two in step or the key will describe a
                        grid that behaves otherwise.
#}
<section class="calendar-board">
    <div class="calendar-board-header">
        <div>
            <h2>{{ heading }}</h2>
            <p class="calendar-board-subtitle">{{ subtitle }}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="calendar-nav-bar">
            <div class="calendar-view-switch" role="group" aria-label="{{ 'cal.view_switch'|trans }}">
                <button type="button" class="calendar-view-button is-on" aria-pressed="true"
                        data-calendar-target="viewButton" data-calendar-view="week"
                        data-action="calendar#setView">{{ 'cal.view_week'|trans }}</button>
                <button type="button" class="calendar-view-button" aria-pressed="false"
                        data-calendar-target="viewButton" data-calendar-view="month"
                        data-action="calendar#setView">{{ 'cal.view_month'|trans }}</button>
            </div>
            {# ⚠️ Arrows, not the words. With the view switch added, "Semaine
               précédente" and "Semaine suivante" spelled out pushed the nav bar onto
               THREE rows at 1440px — measured on the live page, not guessed. The
               words survive as the accessible name and the tooltip, and the chip
               between the arrows already says which week or month is on screen. #}
            <button class="btn btn-secondary calendar-nav-step" type="button"
                    data-calendar-target="previousButton"
                    data-action="calendar#previous"
                    title="{{ 'cal.prev_week'|trans }}" aria-label="{{ 'cal.prev_week'|trans }}"><span aria-hidden="true">‹</span></button>
            {# ⚠️ `aria-live`: the arrows and the view switch replace the whole grid
               without moving focus, so without it a screen reader is told nothing at
               all changed. The chip is the one element that names the new period. #}
            <div class="week-chip" aria-live="polite" data-calendar-target="periodLabel">{{ 'cal.week_label'|trans }}</div>
            <button class="btn btn-secondary" type="button"
                    data-action="calendar#today">{{ 'cal.today'|trans }}</button>
            <button class="btn btn-secondary calendar-nav-step" type="button"
                    data-calendar-target="nextButton"
                    data-action="calendar#next"
                    title="{{ 'cal.next_week'|trans }}" aria-label="{{ 'cal.next_week'|trans }}"><span aria-hidden="true">›</span></button>
        </div>
    </div>

    {% if venue_context|default(null) %}
        {% include 'site/_venue_context.html.twig' with {
            venue_context: venue_context,
            list_route: venue_route,
            list_query: venue_query|default({}),
            tile_attrs: 'data-calendar-target="viewLink"',
        } only %}
    {% endif %}

    {# ⚠️ `data-calendar-week-only` marks chrome that describes the WEEK grid. In
       the month view this strip repeats what every day cell already says, and the
       legend below names marks that are not on screen — a key to a map nobody is
       looking at. The machine page's second strip is NOT marked: there it sits under
       a heading of its own and is the location's weekly hours, not the grid's. #}
    <div class="week-hours-strip" data-calendar-week-only
         data-calendar-target="weekHours" aria-label="{{ 'cal.aria_week_hours'|trans }}"></div>

    <div class="calendar-scroll-area" aria-label="{{ 'cal.aria_grid'|trans }}">
        <div class="agenda-grid" data-calendar-target="grid"></div>
    </div>

    {# ⚠️ **The green dot means different things on the two kinds of page.** A green
       cell is an hour the location is OPEN; on a page you can book from, an empty one
       is also an hour you may take, which is what "Disponible" says. On the read-only
       calendar nothing can be taken, so the same word promises a control that is not
       there — the review's finding. Same dot, honest word for each page. #}
    <div class="calendar-legend-modern" data-calendar-week-only aria-label="{{ 'cal.aria_legend'|trans }}">
        {# 🔴 **`booking|default(true)` was ALWAYS true.** Twig's `default` fires on any
           *empty* value and `false` is empty, so passing `booking: false` resolved to
           `true` and the read-only calendar kept saying "Disponible". This is the
           identical bug `_venue_context.html.twig` documents for `allow_all` — the
           second time it has shipped in this codebase. `is not defined` is the test
           that distinguishes "not passed" from "passed as false". #}
        <span class="legend-dot free">{{ (booking is not defined or booking ? 'cal.legend_free' : 'cal.legend_open')|trans }}</span>
        <span class="legend-dot reserved">{{ 'cal.legend_reserved'|trans }}</span>
        <span class="legend-dot mine">{{ 'cal.legend_mine'|trans }}</span>
        <span class="legend-dot closed">{{ 'kiosk.machine_closed'|trans }}</span>
        <span class="legend-dot out">{{ 'cal.legend_offhours'|trans }}</span>
        <span class="legend-dot cancelled">{{ 'cal.legend_cancelled'|trans }}</span>
    </div>
</section>
